# This repo is public. Everything here is about that.

# State records every managed value in clear, and gains secrets the moment any
# resource has one. It belongs in R2, never here.
*.tfstate
*.tfstate.*
*.tfstate.backup
.terraform/
crash.log
crash.*.log

# terraform.tfvars IS committed, and that is a deliberate reversal.
#
# The rule below was written assuming tfvars is where credentials live. It is
# not: every credential comes from op.env via `op run`, and what is left is an
# SSH PUBLIC key, DKIM PUBLIC keys, DNS records that are world-readable by
# definition, and two flags.
#
# Leaving it ignored made the defaults the effective configuration on any
# machine that had not been used before — so a plan from a second laptop offered
# to destroy the apex MX, both DKIM records and all three relay CNAMEs, and to
# downgrade DMARC to p=none. A plan that differs by machine is not a plan.
#
# ⚠ Nothing secret goes in it. If that ever stops being true, the fix is to move
# the secret to op.env, not to ignore the file again.
*.tfvars
*.tfvars.json
!*.tfvars.example
!terraform.tfvars

# Plan files contain full resource values, including sensitive ones.
*.tfplan
*.tfplan.*

# Never.
*.pem
*.key
*_rsa
*_ed25519

# op.env IS committed. It holds op:// references, not values, and resolving one
# needs an unlocked 1Password account the file knows nothing about. Listed here
# so that adding a blanket *.env rule later doesn't silently hide it.
!op.env

# The lock file IS committed — it pins provider checksums, and that is the
# point of it. Not ignored, listed here so nobody "tidies" it away.
!.terraform.lock.hcl
